Z||000007||BV MotorSports||06-22-2001||01:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steven:<BR><B>There was some "hot rod" style magazine out in the last year that had a guy with a Pontiac 455 put in a Fiero. Seem like he had to use the Front wheel drive set up from a old Olds Toronado.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I believe there are 2 455 v8 fieros running around. One in Canada and the other is in Virginia. <BR>There is also a 501cid (think thats right) v8 caddy powered Fiero in Stuarts Draft Va.<BR>Ownened by Nesbit and Associates. I have personally seen this car AND its N20 Pro fogger system on it!<P>Steven<P><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||06-22-2001||02:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steven:<BR><B>There was some "hot rod" style magazine out in the last year that had a guy with a Pontiac 455 put in a Fiero. Seem like he had to use the Front wheel drive set up from a old Olds Toronado.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This  one was also longitudinally mounted on a custom cradle with a fully custom rear suspension.  I have the article at home, somewhere.  It runs 11s in the 1/4.||||reg||1
Z||000009||rockcrawl||06-22-2001||03:30 PM||||Gary Zumalt sold a BBC Fiero kit before he went out of business a few years ago. It was only like $100 more than his SBC kit. I have some pics somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||06-22-2001||03:43 PM||||The huge caddies were referred to as 500 cid though the might not actually displace that.<BR>There is one rumored to be in my neck of the woods too, though I can't prove it.<BR>If I remember correctly, Big blocks and small blocks use the same transmission bolt pattern so a small block adapter plate would also bolt the two together. however you would need a flywheel and would have to figure out how to wedge the larger (outside dimensions) motor into the car. Not to mention that a big block weighs (again, if I'm remembering this correctly) about 200 lbs more than a small block (making it about twice the weight of the 4 cyl)||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cheever2||06-22-2001||03:53 PM||||Hugh has a big'un, but it's longitudinally mounted.  Nice setup, and I think he said it's 345 horses.  Show us another pic of that, Hugh!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000012||Avery Montembeault||06-22-2001||03:56 PM||||I had a buddy show me some pictures of a 454 fiero once, and it was mounted in the normal place, anybody else see this?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Pontiaddict||06-22-2001||08:51 PM||||I had to rush the last reply because I had to get off of the computer.<P>I believe that Zumwalt did a few big block Fieros (don't hold it against me if i'm wrong though) but I don't think it is (or was) available in an aftermarket kit. If you are only looking for 350 hp then you would be better off using a small block, all you are going to get with a big block at that hp is way to much parts breaking torque.||||reg||1
